{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you come to the land of your dwellings</b> – After He promised the children that they would come to the land, He completed for them the law of the offerings, that they should bring libations when they come to the land. And perhaps this was now to comfort them and assure them, for they were in despair, saying, who knows what will happen after a long time, at the end of forty years, and whether the children too will sin. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, saw fit to comfort them, for by commanding them in the commandments of the land He assured them that it is revealed before Him that they will come and inherit it. And behold, He commanded concerning libations in the land when they bring burnt offerings and sacrifices, for in the wilderness they were not obligated in libations except for the daily offering alone, concerning which it is stated: \"And a drink offering of a quarter hin of wine for the one lamb\" (Exodus 29:40), for there it is stated: \"At the entrance of the Tent of Meeting before YHWH, where I will meet with you there, to speak with you there\" (ibid. v. 42). And likewise the princes at their dedication did not bring libations. And our Sages disagreed about this: some said in Sifrei (Shelach 107): the verse comes to teach you that they were not obligated in libations except from their entry into the land onward, etc.; and some said that the individual was not obligated in libations except from their entry into the land onward. And He further completed the law of the priests and commanded concerning the dough offering (חלה), which was not practiced in the wilderness. For the heave offering (תרומה) and the tithe, it is known that they were not practiced there, for they were from the produce of the gentile and outside the land. And there is yet another distinction regarding the dough offering according to the opinion of our Sages (Ketubot 25a), that they became obligated in the dough offering when they entered the land immediately, but were not obligated in tithes and in the heave offering until after inheritance and settlement."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The phrase מרוח הגוי is translated literally 'from the produce of the gentile'; it refers to grain grown by non-Israelites, which is exempt from these obligations."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:05:50.798426460Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}